WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.490 --> 00:05.920
So we talked about the 80/20 approach, but there are other tools we can use
when we think about prioritisation.

2
00:06.250 --> 00:09.470
And one of the most effective is called the prioritisation matrix.

3
00:09.960 --> 00:15.400
What is a prioritisation matrix? In this approach, we're thinking about
all the different nodes or endpoints of our tree —

4
00:15.400 --> 00:21.600
all the issues and analyses we're looking to investigate —
and we're plotting those on a two-by-two matrix, or grid.

5
00:21.990 --> 00:27.520
And the vertical dimension of this is impact,
and the horizontal dimension is feasibility.

6
00:27.880 --> 00:33.400
Now, for each of these dimensions, we're not always looking to specifically quantify
exactly what the impact or feasibility is.

7
00:33.400 --> 00:39.400
In often cases, what we're really trying to get to is:
what's the relative degree of impact? Is it small, medium, large, if you will?

8
00:39.400 --> 00:43.960
On a feasibility perspective, you might think about

9
00:44.330 --> 00:48.260
how much time or how many resources this might take to approach.

10
00:48.840 --> 00:53.400
And when you do this approach, you may want to think about:
if something is high impact and very feasible —

11
00:53.400 --> 00:57.280
well, that we know we're going to have right at the top of our list of things to do.

12
00:57.640 --> 01:01.950
If something is low impact and it's very hard to do, that's the bottom of the list.

13
01:02.310 --> 01:08.200
And things in the other two boxes — if they are high impact but also aren't quite feasible —
that's where the challenge comes.

14
01:08.200 --> 01:13.200
And you want to think about: are there ways to make it easier to do?
Can you relax some of the constraints

15
01:13.200 --> 01:17.790
you've perhaps defined in terms of your problem,

16
01:17.890 --> 01:22.400
in order to get at that? And how confident are you that you need to do that
in order to get to the right solution?

17
01:22.920 --> 01:28.400
And if things are low impact but they're also very easy to do,
that's where you want to think about:

18
01:28.400 --> 01:34.200
hey, are there some quick wins you could do early in that problem-solving process,
to actually show progress and help get you to the answer quicker?

19
01:34.200 --> 01:37.400
Some of the challenges that can arise when using the prioritisation matrix

20
01:37.700 --> 01:42.560
can typically come from the degree of precision that's involved.
And so if you're thinking about

21
01:43.560 --> 01:49.990
how confident you need to be in your relative positioning of the impact or the feasibility —
if you're problem solving in the moment,

22
01:50.340 --> 01:56.400
you might not have a lot of time to define exactly what the inputs to that are
and get to a very precise numerical solution.

23
01:56.400 --> 02:02.400
But you might be able to say: hey, on a T-shirt sizing —
is it small, medium, large? Or high versus medium versus low?

24
02:02.400 --> 02:07.010
On a relative basis, how can I come up with a very quick relative placement?

25
02:07.530 --> 02:13.400
And when you're thinking about a longer-term project, that's typically where
you do want to spend a little more time up front discussing:

26
02:13.400 --> 02:18.200
well, when we say feasibility, what are the relevant components that drive that?

27
02:18.200 --> 02:23.070
When we're thinking about impact, can we actually agree,
and do some analysis, to more precisely quantify

28
02:23.270 --> 02:29.410
what that impact is — so we actually get a good ranked order
of the different priorities.
